<!--
    By-value aggregates in Objective-C type encodings, and global blocks.

    Issue: an ivar or argument whose encoding is a struct by value
    (`{CGRect={CGPoint=dd}{CGSize=dd}}`) or an array (`[4i]`) decoded to a void
    placeholder, so `-[Shape area]` read `*(double *)&self->frame[0x10] *
    *(double *)&self->frame[0x18]`, `-[Shape countAt:]` indexed
    `self->counts[(arg1 & 3) * 4]` by hand, and `-[Shape setFrame:]` lost its
    prototype altogether (`void f(unsigned long a0, ..., struct_0 *a4)`). A
    global block literal in `__const` was an anonymous word and its invoke
    function a `sub_`.

    Fix: `encoding.rs` decodes `{...}` through `struct_type` (the quoted member
    names an ivar encoding carries, nested structs flattened) and `[n T]` to an
    array; `kuna_objcblocks.rs` types every `_NSConcreteGlobalBlock` literal as
    `Block_layout`, names its invoke `block_invoke_<addr>` and types it from the
    descriptor's signature.

    Pass 1 (`objc off`) is the bug; pass 2 (`objc on`) the fix: `(self->frame).width
    * (self->frame).height`, `self->counts[arg1 & 3]`, `CGRect arg1` placed in
    d0..d3, `long block_invoke_10000071c(Block_layout *block,long arg1)`.
-->
